---
import SupplierLayout from '@/layouts/SupplierLayout.astro';
import { MATTRESS_SPECS } from '@/data/specs';
import { supplierTitle } from '@/lib/seo';

const title = supplierTitle('Mattress');
const terms = [
  { label: 'Minimum order', value: '50 pieces per style. Sizes can be mixed in one order.' },
  { label: 'Firmness', value: 'Medium firm as standard. Softer or firmer is a comfort-layer change, not a spring change.' },
  { label: 'Sampling', value: 'One sample per build. Confirm the surface feel before bulk runs.' },
  { label: 'Lead time', value: 'Quoted per order. Spring units and cut fabric are scheduled separately.' },
  { label: 'Sizes', value: '110 / 120 / 150 / 180 × 200 cm as standard. US, UK and AU bed sizes cut on request.' },
  { label: 'Payment', value: 'Terms come with the quotation and depend on order value and destination.' },
];
---

<SupplierLayout
  title={title}
  description="Hotel mattresses built to your brief: pocket spring, double-sided and topper builds, 110 to 180 cm wide, tested to 16 CFR 1633. MOQ 50 pcs."
  h1="Hotel mattress supplier & custom manufacturer"
  breadcrumb="Hotel Mattress Supplier"
  serviceName="Hotel mattress supply and manufacturing"
  serviceType="Hotel mattress supply and manufacturing"
  specs={MATTRESS_SPECS}
  images={['hotel-mattress-supplier-hero']}
  specLead="Spring unit and fire barrier are the same across widths. What changes with size is the coil count and the cutting plan."
  terms={terms}
  categoryHref="/hotel-mattress-protectors/"
  categoryLabel="Mattress protectors"
>
  <p>
    Hotel mattresses are bought to a brief, not to a preference. A project has bed widths, a
    deck height, a budget per room and a fire standard to meet before anyone talks about how the
    bed feels. We have them built to that brief and ship FOB or CIF, whichever suits your
    forwarder.
  </p>
  <p>
    We make the bed linen, towels and bathrobes at our own plant in Jimo. Mattresses come from specialist
    mattress factories we buy from to our specification, and the flame-retardant barrier comes
    from a nonwoven mill that supplies the same barrier to those factories. The test reports are
    issued to those plants. What you get from us is one order, one contract and one point of
    contact for claims, with the reports and prototype description that match the order.
  </p>
  <p>
    The buyers we work with are usually procurement teams, project contractors and distributors
    furnishing a floor at a time. Single-mattress orders are not what this line is set up for.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Four builds</h2>
  <p>
    Pocket spring is the base. Coils sit in individual fabric pockets, so weight stays under the
    sleeper instead of spreading across the bed. We use 1.9 mm wire on the narrower widths and
    2.0 mm where the span is wider.
  </p>
  <p>
    Independent spring is the same idea with individually pocketed coils. On a double or king,
    one person turning over does not move the other side of the mattress. For a property that
    gets complaints about partner disturbance, this is the build that answers it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Double-sided means the same construction on both faces. If your housekeeping schedule turns
    mattresses rather than replacing them, say so at inquiry — the build has to survive being
    flipped on a regular cycle, and that changes what goes inside.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mattress with topper puts a separate comfort layer over the spring unit. Projects that want
    a softer surface without replacing the core tend to land here. Our price list lists this one
    under its own model name, which translates awkwardly; ask for the topper build and we will
    know what you mean.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Sizes and depth</h2>
  <p>
    Standard widths are 110, 120, 150 and 180 cm, all 200 cm long, in 20, 25 or 30 cm depth.
    That covers single, small double, double and king in most of Europe and Asia.
  </p>
  <p>
    US queen is 60 × 80 in and king is 76 × 80 in. Those are not a straight conversion from the
    metric widths, so they get their own cutting plan. If your drawings are in inches, send them
    in inches. Converting twice is where mistakes come from.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Firmness</h2>
  <p>
    Medium firm is the default, and it is what most hotel briefs ask for. If a project specifies
    softer or firmer, we change the comfort layers, not the spring unit. That keeps the support
    characteristics stable while the surface feel moves.
  </p>
  <p>
    What we cannot do is match another manufacturer's firmness by name. "Medium" is not a
    standard. Send us the mattress the project is replacing, or a sample from the specifier, and
    we will match it by feel.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Fire compliance</h2>
  <p>
    For US projects the standard is 16 CFR 1633, and it is not optional. Our barrier is 220 g
    fire-retardant cotton and 90 g fire-retardant foam over the spring unit. The build has been
    tested to that standard at SGS Shunde, report SDFTS25002706R05_EN. Three specimens passed
    open-flame testing without a foundation. The report comes with your quote.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tested to and compliant are different words, and buyers should know the difference. The
    test covers a prototype, and that report was run on one size: 99 × 191 × 25 cm. It does not
    state a size range. For any other size we ask the manufacturer to confirm in writing that
    the build falls inside the tested prototype before we quote. A report with no size does not
    cover an order.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sets are produced in China to that build. Under 16 CFR 1633 the importer keeps the
    report, the prototype description and a certification on file; on FOB or CIF terms that
    importer is normally you or your broker, not us. Every US order ships with the report and a
    signed factory statement that the build follows the tested prototype. If your compliance
    team has its own template for that statement, send it and we will have it signed before
    production.
  </p>
  <p>
    US, UK and EU standards are not interchangeable. A 1633 test says nothing about BS 7177 or
    EN 597. If the project is outside the US, tell us the standard at inquiry stage. Better to
    know early.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the standard actually tests, and what a passing report does and does not cover, is set
    out in
    <a class="font-semibold underline" href="/guides/16-cfr-1633-hotel-mattress/">
      16 CFR 1633 for hotel mattresses
    </a>.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">Shipping a mattress order</h2>
  <p>
    Mattresses compress and roll for container freight. That changes the packing plan, not the
    finished dimensions — once opened, the mattress recovers to the spec on the drawing. Tell us
    if the property needs them delivered flat and stored before installation; rolled units need
    a recovery window and that has to fit the installation schedule.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mattress protectors usually go out with the same order. Same container, same delivery
    window, and the property is not left wrapping mattresses in plastic for a month.
  </p>

  <h2 class="pt-4 text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What to send</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
    <li>Bed widths and depths, per room type, and the count of each.</li>
    <li>Build: pocket spring, independent spring, double-sided, or with topper.</li>
    <li>Firmness target, or the mattress being replaced.</li>
    <li>Fire standard, and the country the goods are going to.</li>
    <li>Delivery month and destination port.</li>
  </ul>
</SupplierLayout>
